\begin{abstract}
 % The rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) underscores their growing importance, yet their factual accuracy remains a significant challenge. Robust evaluation is critical, but existing benchmarks face hurdles such as rapid performance saturation, limiting their effectiveness in differentiating models. Furthermore, current factuality assessments often prioritize question complexity (e.g., multi-hop reasoning), largely overlooking the crucial ability of LLMs to perform structured data retrieval directly from their internal parametric knowledge – generating answers as tuples, i.e., tabular data with multiple attributes and records. This paper introduces RelationalFactQA, a novel benchmark specifically designed to evaluate this under-explored dimension of LLM factuality. RelationalFactQA features natural language questions across diverse general knowledge topics, paired with annotated answers representing tabular data curated from high-quality sources. 

 % Our experimental results demonstrate that while newer and larger models exhibit improved performance as expected, RelationalFactQA shows that even the most capable LLMs struggle to retrieve accurate tabular answers from their parametric knowledge, failing to achieve quality scores above 50\%. These findings highlight current limitations in structured factual retrieval and establish RelationalFactQA as a challenging and useful tool for measuring future progress in LLM evaluation.
Factuality in Large Language Models (LLMs) is a persistent challenge. Current benchmarks often assess short factual answers, overlooking the critical ability to generate structured, multi-record tabular outputs from parametric knowledge. We demonstrate that this relational fact retrieval is substantially more difficult than isolated point-wise queries, even when individual facts are known to the model, exposing distinct failure modes sensitive to output dimensionality (e.g., number of attributes or records). To systematically evaluate this under-explored capability, we introduce RelationalFactQA, a new benchmark featuring diverse natural language questions (paired with SQL) and gold-standard tabular answers, specifically designed to assess knowledge retrieval in a structured format. RelationalFactQA enables analysis across varying query complexities, output sizes, and data characteristics. Our experiments reveal that even state-of-the-art LLMs struggle significantly, not exceeding 25\% factual accuracy in generating relational outputs, with performance notably degrading as output dimensionality increases. These findings underscore critical limitations in current LLMs' ability to synthesize structured factual knowledge and establish RelationalFactQA as a crucial resource for measuring future progress in LLM factuality.
\end{abstract}

