    \section{Conclusions and Potential Future Directions}
\label{Sec: Conclusions}
In this paper, we propose the RI-WuR-UAC protocol, a novel approach for UAV-assisted clustering in WuR-enabled IoT networks. The protocol is designed to achieve low latency, reliable data transmission, and energy efficiency in ultra-low power consumption applications of IoT. It offers a high degree of flexibility in accommodating varying network sizes while enhancing reliability and minimizing SN energy consumption. The RI-WuR-UAC protocol is analytically modeled using the $M/G/1/2$ queuing framework to derive expressions for key performance metrics, including channel busyness probability, probability of successful cluster formation, average SN energy consumption, and average transmission delay.

We introduce three data flow models tailored for network scenarios with light traffic, heavy traffic, and varying traffic loads, each incorporating a distinct channel access approach: CCA clustering for light traffic, CSMA-CA clustering for dense traffic, and ADP clustering for variable traffic conditions. Moreover, SCM clustering, a contention-free protocol, is used as a benchmark to evaluate the performance of the proposed contention-based clustering MAC protocols. Simulation results demonstrate that all three proposed protocols outperform SCM clustering in terms of reducing the probability of TSN drop from cluster formation and lowering average energy consumption, albeit at the cost of longer transmission delays, i.e., average delay.

Among the proposed contention-based protocols, CCA clustering is best suited for applications requiring both lower transmission delay and high energy efficiency, where a moderate collision probability is acceptable. Conversely, CSMA-CA clustering and ADP clustering are preferable when packet delivery reliability is of greater importance. Between these two, ADP clustering outperforms CSMA-CA clustering in terms of transmission delay and energy consumption. For example, from our results, for a cluster size of 50 SNs, the probability of successful cluster formation is 0.0003 for SCM clustering, 0.3522 for CCA clustering, 0.2894 for CSMA-CA clustering, and 0.3002 for ADP clustering. The average energy consumption by an SN, measured in milli-Joules (mJ), is 3.898 for SCM clustering, 0.9603 for CCA clustering, 1.0305 for CSMA-CA clustering, and 1.134 for ADP-WuR. The respective transmission delays, measured in milliseconds (ms), are 8.718 for SCM clustering, 41.76 for CCA clustering, 45.22 for CSMA-CA clustering, and 44.02 for ADP clustering.

The RI-WuR-UAC protocol builds upon our previously proposed EEUCH \cite{Luqman_EEUCH} routing protocol, addressing several challenges faced by EEUCH at the MAC layer. These challenges include how SNs access the shared wireless medium, avoid collisions and a reliable handshake during the setup phase, ensure successful cluster formation, and determine the time and energy required for cluster formation.

Several promising directions for future research remain, which our future work will explore. One key area is determining the optimal UAV-to-SN ratio to ensure efficient data collection in UAV-assisted IoT networks. This includes assessing how many SNs can be effectively managed by a single UAV while meeting desired reliability, latency, and energy efficiency targets. Another important aspect is network-wide energy consumption. While the RI-WuR-UAC protocol addresses the energy consumption of the TSN in a single round, it assumes similar energy usage for other SNs in the cluster. However, the energy consumed by the UAV itself is a critical factor that must be considered to fully optimize network efficiency.
\end{comment}

\section{Conclusions and Potential Future Directions}
\label{Sec: Conclusions}
This paper proposed the RI-WuR-UAC protocol for UAV-assisted clustering in WuR-enabled IoT networks, designed to provide low latency, energy efficiency, and reliable data transmission in ultra-low power IoT applications. The protocol was analytically modeled using the $M/G/1/2$ queuing framework, yielding key performance metrics such as channel busyness probability, successful cluster formation probability, average energy consumption, and transmission delay.

Three distinct data flow models were introduced to handle varying traffic conditions: CCA clustering for light traffic, CSMA-CA clustering for dense traffic, and ADP clustering for variable loads. These models were evaluated against the contention-free SCM protocol. Simulation results showed that the proposed RI-WuR-UAC protocol significantly reduces SN energy consumption and improves cluster formation success rates compared to SCM, though at the cost of increased transmission delays. For example, in a network of 50 SNs, CCA clustering showed the highest energy efficiency with an average consumption of 0.9603 mJ per SN, while ADP clustering achieved the lowest transmission delay among contention-based protocols.

The RI-WuR-UAC protocol also addressed critical challenges in our earlier EEUCH routing protocol, particularly in medium access, collision avoidance, and handshaking during cluster formation. Future research will focus on optimizing the UAV-to-SN ratio for efficient data collection, ensuring reliability, low latency, and energy efficiency. A well-calibrated UAV-to-SN ratio can mitigate the increased transmission delays observed in the proposed data flow models compared to the SCM protocol. Furthermore, UAV energy consumption must be fully accounted for in order to optimize network-wide performance.

