\subsection{Time intervals $t$ of timing signal in dynamic calibration}
During the inertial motion capture process, we use a timing signal $S_t$ with an interval of $t$to run the TIC network. Because in general, the changing of the calibration parameters is slow (e.g., coordinate drift of $0.1$ deg/s), allowing for updates at a lower frequency to avoid unnecessary computational cost. We tested the calibration accuracy under different $t$ settings. As shown in the table~\ref{tab:time intervals settings}, when $t \leq 2$ seconds, there is no significant difference between OME and AME. However, as $t$ increases, OME begins to rise, and a notable decline in performance is observed in both OME and AME at $t = 20$ seconds. Consequently, we have chosen $t = 1$ seconds to achieve a trade-off between performance and computational cost.
